Here, now I will include the phenomenon of formation of dendrites since their occurence is driven by factors like- some crystallographic directions grow faster, atoms attach more easily along preferred directions and interface energy is anisotropic. 
So the interface does NOT advance uniformly. As until now, orientation only affected growth speed while treating all neighbouring directions similarly. Here the preferential selection makes some portion to grow faster, protruding into undercooled liquid zone and thus extending and forming branches further simultaneously. 
Until before, grain orientation only affected the growth probability. But now, it may happen that the neighbouring directions even withing the same grain may grow differently. like- north neighbour may grow faster than a diagonal neighbour. 
Dendrites arise because interface velocity depends on angle:

v(θ)=V(1+ϵcos(nθ))

where:

v(θ) = growth velocity in direction θ
ϵ = anisotropy strength
n = crystal symmetry

This is the mathematical foundation of anisotropic growth. I didn't referred to the code to solve this equation directly. Rather we will just emulate and assign specific directions for now. 
Also, the resolution of the grid will also be increased now to 300*300 from earlier 100*100 for better visualization.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TLDR : if a grain is aligned with the preferred orientation, it will grow uniformly in all directions as its growth is preferred by being more aligned. Now, we not only affect the growth of grains based on their orientation, but also the specific direction of growth as well. 
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Why V1 and V2? : 
V1 is having a condition for hard anisotropy where certain directions for grain growth are completely prohibited as cos gets the value 0. this is responsible for getting that checkered pattern in grains where those diagonal grains are more accessible ans the grain grows in those directions only. This is fixed by using soft anisotropy condition as replaced in V2 where anisotropy factor will never reduce to 0 and hence all directions remain accessible when probability is calculated. 

Optional code of inclusion of captured cells and prevent rechecking all is also in the directory.Helps to save time and computation. 

